1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A generating function turns a whole sequence into one object: a series whose coefficients are the terms.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Write the terms on powers of x: the term at position zero on x to the zero, the next on x to the one, and so on forever.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
x is a placeholder, not a number. Nothing is ever evaluated, so nothing has to converge.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Reading a coefficient back is the whole art: the coefficient of x to the n in G of x is a sub n.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Five closed forms cover nearly every question. One over one minus x is the sequence of all ones.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Divide by one minus x once more and the coefficients become 1, 2, 3, 4: choosing with repetition.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
One over one minus x squared puts a one only on the even powers; replacing x by x squared filters by parity.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Multiplying by x shifts the sequence one place right, and differentiating multiplies the term at position n by n.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
GATE 2016 Set 1 Question 26 asks for the coefficient of x to the twelfth in a cube of a series that starts at x cubed.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Each factor chooses a power of at least three, so the question is how many triples of numbers at least three add to twelve.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
There are ten triples: one with all three equal, six with a repeated three, and three with the six in a different place.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The series way is faster: take x cubed out of each factor, then read the coefficient of x cubed in one over one minus x, cubed.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Take one over one minus x minus x squared and expand it. The coefficients come out 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
That is the Fibonacci sequence, and the denominator is the recurrence written in a different alphabet.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Multiply both sides by the denominator, then match the coefficient of x to the n on the two sides.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
You get a sub n equals a sub n minus one plus a sub n minus two. Every rational generating function hides a recurrence this way.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
GATE 2018 Question 1: the sequence two n plus three. Split it, because a sum of sequences is a sum of generating functions.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Three times all ones is three over one minus x, and two n is two x over one minus x squared. Together, three minus x over one minus x squared.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
GATE 2017 Set 2 Question 47: one plus z over one minus z cubed. Expand it and the coefficients are 1, 4, 9, 16.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
So a three minus a zero is sixteen minus one, which is fifteen. Both questions fall to one table of atoms.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
GATE 2022 Question 26: the term is n plus one at odd positions and one at even positions. Split the sequence in two.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
The even half is all ones on even powers: one over one minus x squared. The odd half is two, four, six, eight on odd powers.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
The options use one over one minus x, so rewrite the even half as one over one minus x minus x over one minus x squared.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
That gives x plus x cubed over one minus x squared squared, plus one over one minus x: option A.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Now run it backwards. Take a sub n equals five a sub n minus one minus six a sub n minus two, with the first two terms one and two.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Multiply by x to the n and add over every n from two upward. Each shifted sum is the original series times a power of x.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Collect: G times one minus five x plus six x squared equals one minus three x. The numerator factorises and cancels.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
G is one over one minus two x, so the term is exactly two to the n — and the three-to-the-n part is gone.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Count the balanced bracketings of n pairs. Splitting at the outermost pair gives C of x equals one plus x times C of x squared.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
That is a quadratic in C. Solving it gives C equals one plus or minus the square root of one minus four x, all over two x.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
The plus sign gives one over x plus one plus two x: not a power series at all, because it blows up at zero.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The minus sign gives 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42. The constant term decides the branch, because a zero must equal the first term.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
An ordinary generating function counts without regard to order: the powers of x simply add up sizes.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
When order matters the objects are labelled, and the right object divides each term by n factorial.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
The test is clean: n factorial has no useful ordinary generating function, but under the exponential form it is one over one minus x.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
So the ordinary form answers how many ways to choose, the exponential form how many ways to arrange.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Procedure: name the atoms you have, combine them with the sum and product rules, then extract the coefficient you need.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Trap one: forgetting the shift that x to the k puts on the answer. Trap two: writing n choose k where it should be n plus k minus one choose k.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Trap three: substituting x equal to one. Trap four: taking the wrong root, or forgetting that the constant term is a zero.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Then verify: expand the first four terms of your closed form and compare them with the sequence.

